WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.300
Start this week with a moment that did not happen. On May eighteenth, Donald Trump said he had called off a planned strike on Iran because negotiations had become serious. There was no explosion in that story, but it was not light. Sometimes the weight of a week sits in the button that was not pressed.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:30.800
By the weekend, the language around Iran and the Strait of Hormuz had moved again. Trump said a deal was largely negotiated. Later reporting showed details still coming into view. The point was not one optimistic sentence. It was that the strait, tankers, military pressure, and diplomatic channels were all on the same table.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:47.100
That same day, Kyiv did not get a quiet night. Russia launched a large drone and missile attack, and Ukraine said the Oreshnik hypersonic ballistic missile was used. The images are fire, smoke, damaged buildings, and people forced out into the street. Here, war is not an arrow on a map. It is a street losing sleep.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:01:05.700
On May twenty-second, Taiwan answered questions about whether a planned US arms sale had been paused, saying it had not received such a notice. The background was a US hearing where officials described some foreign military sales being delayed because of ammunition needs tied to Iran. There was no front-line fire in this story, but there were promises, inventories, and schedules.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:31.200
The economic and technology stories were busy too. The European Union approved a tariff arrangement with the United States, capping many export duties near fifteen percent. Taiwan prosecutors investigated high-performance AI servers allegedly moving toward China. In the United States, Trump disclosed more than thirty-six hundred stock trades in the first quarter, including companies such as Nvidia, Apple, and Boeing. Some news appears first not as a scene, but as a form, a chip, or a tariff line.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:50.100
The weather stories this week were also concrete. Heavy rain and flooding in Hunan, Hubei, Guizhou and nearby areas killed people and forced large evacuations. In India, a heat wave pushed electricity demand past two hundred seventy gigawatts. Climate news often sounds huge. On the ground, it is water entering the street, power running short, and nights that stay hot.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:02:20.000
Over the weekend, a gas explosion tore through a coal mine in Changzhi, Shanxi. AP reported that, as of May twenty-fourth, at least eighty-two people were dead, two were missing, and more than one hundred were hospitalized. Japan's Prime Minister Sanae Takaichi sent a message of condolence. South Korean President Lee Jae Myung wrote on X, and India's Prime Minister Narendra Modi also offered condolences to China. This story is heavy because it is not an abstract industrial accident. It is many families suddenly losing someone who was supposed to come home.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:38.100
Put the week together, and the useful pause is not only over which headline was largest. It is over who had to change a life around it. Someone waited for a deal. Someone cleared wreckage. Someone checked an arms-sale schedule. Someone left home through floodwater. Someone never came back from a mine shaft. This is Codex Guanlan. Next week, we keep looking for the people behind the news.
